---
/**
 * Section — who is out there.
 *
 * One number and one globe. The number is how many GitHub stars the repository
 * has; the marks are the stargazers who say on their profile where they are.
 * See docs/section-globe.md for the whole contract.
 *
 * THE SECTION IS EXACTLY ONE VIEWPORT. `section-full-height` sets the floor and
 * the body is a `flex-1` box that centres what it holds, so surplus height on a
 * tall monitor becomes air above and below rather than a gap in the middle —
 * the same construction as `Install.astro`, and the same reason. The floor is a
 * MINIMUM: on a short window the content grows past one screen and the page
 * scrolls instead of clipping.
 *
 * AND IT HOLDS THE READER FOR A BEAT. The section is a `[data-track]` with a
 * `position: sticky` child, the same construction as `VoiceSwitch` and
 * `Install` — see docs/layout.md § "A section that is one viewport and still
 * scrolls". Here it drives NOTHING: there is no scrub, no sequence, and the
 * pinned screen is identical at every scroll position inside it. The pause IS
 * the effect (maintainer, 2026-08-29). A globe that is gone before it has
 * turned once reads as a decoration the page scrolled past; held for a beat it
 * reads as something to look at.
 *
 * WHICH IS WHY THE TRACK IS SHORT. `--track: 180svh` is 0.8 of a screen of
 * waiting — two or three turns of a wheel. The other two tracks are three and
 * four screens because they have a timeline to spend it on; a still picture
 * held that long is a page the reader thinks has stopped responding.
 *
 * AND THE PAUSE HAS TO ANSWER THE SCROLL. A held screen that answers nothing
 * is a page that has stopped working, so `[data-scroll-span]` on the track
 * hands the pinned span to the two things that read it:
 *
 *   the globe    turns half a revolution across the span — a function of the
 *                scroll position, so it runs backwards on the way back and
 *                stands still when the reader does. See `SCROLL_TURN` in
 *                StarGlobe.tsx for why it is a second angle and not a faster
 *                idle rotation: the globe already turns on its own, and motion
 *                that happens anyway cannot report anything.
 *   the marker   `SectionTrack` walks the square round the frame over the same
 *                span, which is where the reader reads how much is left.
 *
 * Both read ONE attribute, so they cannot disagree about how far through the
 * pause the reader is — the defect that would actually be noticed.
 *
 * IT KEEPS `.section-bounds`, unlike the other two pinned sections. They cannot
 * use those classes because their pinned box is a bespoke frame; this one's is
 * the ordinary band, still on the ordinary Container, so wrapping a track
 * around it changes the pacing and not one pixel of the section. The shell's
 * two measurements stay exactly where docs/layout.md puts them.
 *
 * The globe is what makes that work or not, because it is square and therefore
 * as tall as it is wide. It is capped at `min(column width, viewport height)`,
 * so on a wide screen the column governs and on a short one the screen does.
 *
 * THE NUMBER IS TRUE AT THE MOMENT IT IS READ. It is rendered here from the
 * last build, and the browser then asks GitHub for the current figure and moves
 * to it if it has changed. That call is public and anonymous, so it needs no
 * token and no server of ours; if it fails for any reason the built figure
 * stands. See StarGlobe.tsx.
 *
 * THE COPY STATES WHAT IS MISSING, NOT ONLY WHAT IS THERE. Most GitHub profiles
 * carry no location, so most stargazers cannot be placed at all — far more are
 * absent from the globe than are on it. A visualisation that shows seven marks
 * and says nothing invites the reader to believe seven is the number. The
 * standfirst therefore says the count is live, says how many places the globe
 * marks, and says why the rest are not there.
 *
 * The breakdown is deliberately written WITHOUT arithmetic against the live
 * count. "7 places" describes the marks, which come from the same build as the
 * sentence; "24 of 31 could not be placed" would be stale the moment someone
 * stars the repository, which is the one thing this section promises will show
 * up immediately.
 *
 * THE FIGURE IS NOT A HEADLINE. It is set larger than the hero's h1 tops out
 * at, which looks like a contradiction of docs/design.md's "exactly one largest
 * line" and is not: that rule is about display TYPE, and a two-digit number
 * carries a fraction of the ink of a twelve-word sentence at the same size.
 *
 * Widths come from the one Container. No max-width is named in this file —
 * docs/layout.md reserves that for widths.ts and layout.css, and
 * scripts/check-style.mjs enforces it.
 */
import Container from "@/components/Container.astro";
import SectionTrack from "@/components/SectionTrack.astro";
import { StarCount, StarGlobe } from "@/components/globe/StarGlobe";
import stargazers from "@/data/stargazers.json";

const { repo, count, placed, clusters } = stargazers;

const places = placed === 1 ? "one place" : `${clusters.length} places`;
---

{/*
  A BOUNDED SECTION — both of its lines, held an inset inside its own edges.
  See docs/layout.md § "A bounded section".

  It used to carry a single `.section-rule` at the top and rely on its
  neighbour to close it, which is the default arrangement and works everywhere
  except here: the section below is bounded too, so its lines stand an inset
  inside ITS edges and never land on this boundary. The globe therefore ran out
  of the bottom of the screen with nothing drawn under it.

  Adding a closing rule flush with the bottom edge fixes the structure and not
  what the reader sees. This section is exactly one viewport, so a line on its
  bottom edge sits on the bottom edge of the WINDOW — never in the same view as
  the opening line, which is the thing that makes a section read as bounded.
  Framed, both lines stand inside the screen at once and the section reads as
  one object (maintainer, 2026-08-29).
*/}
<section id="stargazers" class="relative">
  {/*
    The scroll track. Its only job is to be tall — the height IS the pause, and
    the sticky child inside it is the one screen anyone sees. `data-scroll-span`
    is what tells `SectionTrack` to read this element's passage through the
    window instead of the pinned box's, which does not move while it is pinned.

    Below the desktop breakpoint, on a short window and under reduced motion the
    track collapses to `auto` and the section is the ordinary block it was —
    see the styles. The height chain is written so that both cases are the same
    chain: this box, a full-height flex column, the Container that fills it.
  */}
  <div data-track data-scroll-span>
    <div data-viewport class="section-full-height flex flex-col">
      <Container
        width="content"
        class="section-inset flex min-h-0 flex-1 flex-col"
      >
        <div
          class="section-bounds section-bounds--fill stargazers-body flex flex-col justify-center"
        >
          {/* `nested`, because `.section-bounds` already sits on the content
              column: a second `content` Container would apply its gutter and
              its max-width again and stand the marker a gutter inside both
              rails. */}
          <SectionTrack nested />

          <div class="grid items-center gap-12 lg:grid-cols-12 lg:gap-10">
            <div class="stargazers-copy lg:col-span-4">
              <p class="stargazers-eyebrow">In the open</p>

              <!-- The unit sits beside the figure on its own baseline rather
                   than in a sentence below it. A number needs to say what it
                   counts, and "31" over "people have starred Personal Jarvis"
                   made the reader hold the figure in mind for a line before
                   finding out. -->
              <p class="stargazers-figure">
                <span class="stargazers-count">
                  <StarCount client:visible repo={repo} value={count} />
                </span>
                <span class="stargazers-unit">GitHub stars</span>
              </p>

              <hr class="stargazers-hr" />

              <p class="stargazers-note">
                Live from GitHub, counted again every time this page loads.
              </p>

              <p class="stargazers-note stargazers-note-muted">
                The globe marks {places}. Most GitHub profiles carry no location
                at all, so most stargazers cannot be placed — and none are
                placed by guesswork.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="stargazers-globe lg:col-span-8">
              <!-- client:visible, not client:load: the globe is most of the way
                   down the page, and a rotation nobody is looking at is a frame
                   budget spent on nothing. -->
              <StarGlobe client:visible clusters={clusters} />
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </Container>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  /* --- The track and its one visible screen -------------------------------
   *
   * `--track` is how long the section holds the reader. They scroll
   * `track - viewport` of distance and the screen does not change, because
   * there is nothing here for that distance to drive — the holding is the
   * point. See the note at the top of this file for why it is 0.8 of a screen
   * and not the three or four the other two tracks take.
   *
   * NO `overflow: hidden` on the viewport, unlike `VoiceSwitch`. There the
   * picture is bled to the frame's edges and has to be cut; here everything is
   * capped against `svh` and fits by construction, so hiding the overflow could
   * only ever hide a fault instead of showing it.
   */
  [data-track] {
    --track: 180svh;

    height: var(--track);
  }

  /* `section-full-height` is on this element in the markup, so the pinned box
     and the fallback block are the same box with one property different: it is
     `height: 100svh` while it pins and `auto` when it does not, over a floor of
     one screen either way. */
  [data-viewport] {
    position: sticky;
    top: 0;
    height: 100svh;
  }

  /*
   * WHERE THE PIN IS DROPPED.
   *
   *   narrow    the grid has collapsed to one column, so the section is two
   *             screens of content and there is nothing to pin
   *   short     below the height at which the section still fits one screen
   *   calm      prefers-reduced-motion. A pause the reader did not ask for is
   *             motion in the sense that matters: the page not answering the
   *             gesture they just made.
   *
   * THE HEIGHT IS 600 AND NOT THE 880 THE OTHER TWO TRACKS USE, and copying
   * theirs is the bug this replaced. `VoiceSwitch` and `Install` give a
   * PHOTOGRAPH whatever height is left over, so below about 880px that slot is
   * a 4:1 letterbox and the picture stops surviving the crop — their number is
   * a fact about a fixed-aspect image in a flex chain. Nothing here works that
   * way: the globe is capped against `svh` and simply gets smaller, and the
   * copy beside it is four short blocks.
   *
   * Measured on the built page at 1440 wide, forcing the un-pinned layout and
   * reading the height the section actually wants: 600, 640, 680, 720, 760,
   * 800, 840, 880, 940 and 1040 all come back fitting exactly one screen with
   * zero overflow, the globe going 219px → 400px across that range. There is
   * no height in it where the section stops fitting, so the borrowed 880 was
   * not a floor — it was switching the pause off on every 1440x900 laptop for
   * no reason anyone could have found in the layout.
   *
   * 600 is therefore the bottom of what was measured rather than a point where
   * something breaks. Below it the section is a landscape phone or a window
   * squashed to a letterbox, and holding a reader still on either is a worse
   * bet than letting them past — `max-width` already takes most of that case.
   *
   * The section is then exactly what it was before the track existed: one
   * ordinary full-height block. Nothing else in the file knows the difference.
   */
  @media (max-width: 1023px), (max-height: 599px), (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    [data-track] {
      height: auto;
    }

    [data-viewport] {
      position: static;
      height: auto;
    }
  }

  /* A floor, not a fixed amount. On a tall monitor the flex box is taller than
     its content and `justify-center` spreads the surplus evenly; on a short
     laptop the floor is all there is and the section grows past one screen,
     which `section-full-height` allows because it sets a minimum. */
  .stargazers-body {
    padding-block: clamp(2rem, 4svh, 4.5rem);
  }

  /*
   * THE COPY STEPS OFF THE RAIL.
   *
   * The rails stand on the edges of the `content` step and the container's
   * padding starts exactly there, so text set flush in the column sits ON the
   * hairline and reads as though it has fallen against the wall.
   *
   * This is an inset within the column, not a second container padding — the
   * width still comes from the one Container, which is what docs/layout.md
   * actually reserves. It applies only from `lg`, where the two-column layout
   * exists; on a phone the column is the whole screen and every pixel of it is
   * needed for the line length.
   */
  @media (min-width: 1024px) {
    .stargazers-copy {
      padding-inline-start: 2.5rem;
    }
  }

  .stargazers-eyebrow {
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: var(--text-caption-upper);
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-caption-upper);
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  .stargazers-figure {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: 0.2em 0.5rem;
    margin-block-start: 1.25rem;
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  /* Bigger than the hero's h1 tops out at, and that is the point: it is the
     only thing in the section apart from the globe, and at the previous size
     it read as a caption next to a picture. Still weight 400 — docs/design.md
     is right that dropping display type to 700 is the fastest way to lose
     this look, and a number this large needs no help being seen. */
  .stargazers-count {
    font-size: clamp(3rem, 2.1rem + 3.4vw, 5.5rem);
    line-height: 1;
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-display-mega);
  }

  .stargazers-unit {
    font-size: var(--text-display-md);
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-display-md);
  }

  .stargazers-hr {
    margin-block-start: 2rem;
    border: 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--hairline);
  }

  .stargazers-note {
    margin-block-start: 1.25rem;
    font-size: var(--text-body-sm);
    color: var(--body);
  }

  .stargazers-note-muted {
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  /*
   * The globe is square, so a cap on its width is a cap on its height too.
   *
   * `100%` alone lets the column decide, which is right until the window is
   * shorter than the column is wide — then a square that fills the column is
   * taller than the screen and the section stops being one viewport. The svh
   * term is not a content width in the sense docs/layout.md forbids: the
   * container still governs through `100%`, and this only stops the graphic
   * outgrowing the screen it has to fit on.
   *
   * The subtraction is the section's own furniture: the two rules, the body's
   * padding at its ceiling, and the nav the page keeps above everything —
   * PLUS `2 x --section-inset`, which is what makes this a framed band rather
   * than a section flush with its edges. docs/layout.md § "The inset is a
   * share" names that term specifically: it is up to 80px at each end, and a
   * viewport-derived cap that forgets it overflows the screen by 160px.
   */
  .stargazers-globe :global(.star-globe) {
    /* A quarter smaller than the column would allow (maintainer, 2026-08-29).
       At full width the globe crowded the copy and left the section with no
       air of its own; the scale is a single number so the next adjustment is
       one edit rather than four. */
    --globe-scale: 0.75;

    width: min(
      calc(100% * var(--globe-scale)),
      calc((100svh - 14rem - 2 * var(--section-inset)) * var(--globe-scale))
    );
    margin-inline: auto;
  }
</style>
